          "text": "Over the last 3 weeks you averaged about **7 hours a night**, but your sleep has been getting shorter, and the last three nights were among your shortest. One caution first: most of the detailed numbers look like they weren't recorded properly.\n\n## Sleep duration (21 nights, Feb 26 – Mar 18)\n\n| Week | Average sleep |\n|---|---|\n| Feb 26 – Mar 4 | ~7.2 h |\n| Mar 5 – Mar 11 | ~7.0 h |\n| Mar 12 – Mar 18 | ~6.7 h |\n\n- **Longest nights:** 8.1 h (Mar 3) and 8.0 h (Mar 10)\n- **Under 6 hours:** Mar 7 (5.9 h) and Mar 17 (5.7 h)\n- **Last 3 nights:** 6.2 h, 5.7 h and 6.25 h. That's the shortest stretch in the period.\n- No naps were recorded.\n\n## Last night (Mar 18)\n- **Sleep score:** 74 (Fair), your best score in the period\n- **Sleep:** 6.25 h, plus about 26 min awake\n- **Sleep stages:** 18.7% deep, 21.3% REM\n- **Average stress during sleep:** 14\n- **Overnight HRV:** 58 ms\n- **Blood oxygen (SpO2):** 95% average, lowest 88%\n\n## ⚠️ The detailed data looks unreliable\nFor every night from Feb 26 to Mar 17 (20 nights), Garmin reports exactly the same values:\n- Sleep score 63\n- Overnight HRV 61 ms\n- Average stress during sleep 19\n- SpO2 94% average, lowest 87%\n- 13 min awake across 2 wake-ups\n- Almost the same stage split (about 16.7% deep, 63% light, 20% REM)\n\nThe recorded bedtimes and wake times are also identical every night, about 5.9 hours apart. On many nights that's shorter than the sleep time reported, which isn't possible. So the hours above may be off too. It looks like Garmin filled in placeholder values, possibly because the watch wasn't worn or didn't sync properly. **I wouldn't read anything into the scores, HRV or sleep stages for those 20 nights.** Last night's numbers do add up and look like a real recording.\n\nIf you open a few of those nights in Garmin Connect, you can check whether they show the same values there. Until then, the main takeaway is that you've been sleeping less over the past few days.",
